"""Reader-facing usage notes kept beside the reference build scripts.

Every code block in index.html has one guide immediately above it. Keep these
notes focused on what to pass, what comes back, and the important limitation.
"""

GUIDES = {
    "Fast I/O CP Template": "Start a C++17 solution with this template. Call fast_io() before reading input, then replace the body of the test-case loop with your solution. The int macro makes ordinary int variables 64-bit; signed main() keeps the required main return type.",
    "Custom Sort & Comparators": "Choose the comparator form that fits your data: lambda or function for sort, operator< for a reusable object order, index sorting when the original array must stay intact, and ComparePQ for a min-heap. A comparator must give a consistent strict order; equal keys must compare false both ways.",
    "GCC Optimization Pragmas": "Place these lines before includes only when compiling with GCC. O3 and loop unrolling request more aggressive optimization; the target line emits AVX2 instructions. Check the judge CPU first, because unsupported instructions make the program fail at runtime.",
    "__int128 Fast I/O & Output": "Use read128() to parse a signed integer larger than long long, and print128(x) to write one. These helpers use getchar()/putchar(), so avoid mixing them with unsynchronized cin/cout. Keep calculations within the signed __int128 range.",
    "Types, Overflow & Float Comparisons": "Use long long for large integer sums and 1LL before a multiplication that could overflow int. Compare floating values with a tolerance chosen for the problem scale, and use long double for extra precision. Cast or promote before arithmetic; assigning an already-overflowed result to a wider type cannot repair it.",
    "C++ <cmath> Functions (exp, ln, root, sin, cos)": "Copy the needed expression for exponential, logarithmic, root, or trigonometric calculations. Angles passed to sin/cos/tan are radians; convert degrees first if needed. Check domains such as log(x) for x>0 and sqrt(x) for x>=0.",
    "Matrix I/O & Grid Helpers": "Read n and m, then choose the numeric matrix or character-grid example. Direction arrays describe neighboring cells; test row and column bounds before indexing a neighbor. The 4-direction arrays handle orthogonal moves and the 8-direction arrays include diagonals.",
    "All Type Converters & Parsers": "Use the matching conversion for numbers, strings, digits, bases, or tokenized input. stoi/stoll expect a valid numeric prefix and can throw on bad input or overflow. For base conversion, validate each digit against the selected base before accumulating the result.",
    "All STL Containers: Operations & Complexity": "Pick a container by required operation: vector for random access, deque for both ends, set/map for ordered lookup, and unordered_set/map for average constant-time lookup. The examples show insertion, deletion, and access syntax. Iterator invalidation and worst-case hash behavior still matter.",
    "All STL Algorithms: Quick Syntax & Uses": "Use these calls on valid iterator ranges, usually [begin,end). Sort before lower_bound, upper_bound, or binary_search; their result is an iterator and may equal end. The numeric helpers need an initial value of the intended type to avoid overflow.",
    "Coordinate Compression (O(N log N))": "Given vector a, sort and deduplicate a copy, then replace each a[i] by its zero-based position in the sorted unique values. Equal inputs receive equal ranks, and the relative order of distinct values is preserved. Keep vals if you need to map a rank back to its original coordinate.",
    "Greatest Common Divisor (GCD)": "Call gcd(a,b) to get the greatest common divisor using Euclid's remainder method. It is useful for reducing fractions or checking coprimality; gcd(a,0) returns a. Prefer nonnegative inputs when using this exact helper.",
    "Least Common Multiple (LCM)": "Call lcm(a,b) for the smallest common multiple; a zero input returns zero. Dividing by gcd before multiplying reduces overflow risk, but the final product must still fit the integer type. Use nonnegative inputs if you need a nonnegative result.",
    "Sieve of Eratosthenes": "Call gprime(n) once to fill is_prime[0..n] and the ascending primes vector. Test primality with is_prime[x] only for x within that range. Preprocessing takes O(n log log n) time and O(n) memory.",
    "Linear Sieve & Fast Factorization (SPF)": "Call linear_sieve() before get_factors(x). The sieve stores each number's smallest prime factor; get_factors returns ascending (prime, exponent) pairs for 1 <= x < MAXV. Set MAXV to cover the largest query and budget memory for the arrays.",
    "Binary Exponentiation with Modulo": "Call binpow(a,b,m) for a^b mod m with b>=0 and m>0. The function normalizes a negative base and uses __int128 products to avoid 64-bit multiplication overflow. It takes O(log b) time and returns a residue in [0,m).",
    "Binary Exponentiation (Standard)": "Call binpow(a,b) to compute an ordinary integer power for b>=0. Repeated squaring takes O(log b) multiplications. This version has no overflow protection, so use it only when the full result fits long long.",
    "Count Set Bits (1s) from 0 to N": "Call numofone(n) to count all 1 bits in the binary representations of integers 0 through n, inclusive. Each bit contributes complete cycles plus a partial cycle, giving O(64) time. The result is __uint128_t; print it with a compatible 128-bit output helper.",
    "Extended Euclidean & Modular Inverse": "ext_gcd(a,b,x,y) writes coefficients satisfying ax+by=gcd(a,b). Call mod_inv(a,m) only when m>1 and gcd(a,m)=1; it returns the inverse in [0,m). The assertion intentionally rejects inputs without an inverse.",
    "Combinatorics: nCr in O(R)": "Call nCr(n,r) for an exact binomial coefficient, with 0<=r<=n; invalid r returns zero. The loop uses symmetry to make at most min(r,n-r) iterations. Intermediate multiplication can overflow even when a later division is exact, so use a wider type or big integers for large n.",
    "Stable Quadratic Equation Solver": "Call solve_quadratic(a,b,c) for real roots of ax^2+bx+c=0. It prints the real solutions, or a no-solution message; near-zero a is handled as a linear equation. EPS controls when coefficients and the discriminant count as zero, so adapt it to your input scale.",
    "Basis Conversion (Base 2 to 16)": "Use the parser to turn a base-b digit string into an unsigned integer and the formatter for the reverse direction, with 2<=b<=16. Digits above 9 use A-F. Check input validity and overflow before relying on a conversion of a long string.",
    "All Binary Operations & Bit Tricks": "Use GCC builtins for bit counts and leading/trailing zeros, and the bit-mask examples for setting, clearing, testing, and enumerating submasks. Choose the ll builtin variant for 64-bit values. Never call clz or ctz with zero; shifts must stay within the type's bit width.",
    "Complete Modulo Sheet & O(1) nCr": "Set prime MOD, then use norm/add/sub/mul for residues and power for modular exponentiation. Call precompute_nCr(max_n) once before nCr_mod(n,r). Fermat inverse requires a nonzero residue; factorial combinations require n<MOD and n within the prepared range.",
    "Adjacency List Input": "Read V vertices and E weighted directed edges into adj[u] as (neighbor, weight) pairs. Vertices here are zero-indexed; uncomment the reverse insertion for an undirected graph. This representation uses O(V+E) memory and is suited to graph traversal and shortest paths.",
    "Edge List Input": "Read each weighted edge as {u,v,w} and retain it in edges. This form is convenient for Bellman-Ford, Kruskal, or repeated edge scans; add both orientations if an algorithm expects directed edges for an undirected input. The example assumes zero-indexed vertex IDs.",
    "DFS Traversal with Graph Input": "Read an undirected 1-indexed graph, then start DFS at every unvisited vertex to cover disconnected components. visited marks each vertex once, so traversal takes O(V+E). Deep paths can exhaust the recursion stack; use an explicit stack for very large graphs.",
    "DFS Function Only (Standalone)": "Pass a starting node, adjacency array, and shared visited vector. The function marks every reachable vertex, so reset or reuse visited according to the task. Call it separately for each component if you need to cover the entire graph; recursion depth may be large.",
    "BFS Traversal with Graph Input": "Build the graph as shown and start BFS from the desired vertex. The queue visits reachable vertices in nondecreasing edge distance; use a distance array if actual levels are needed. Repeat from unvisited vertices to process disconnected components.",
    "BFS Function Only (Standalone)": "Pass the source, adjacency array, and visited vector to traverse its reachable component. Each vertex enters the queue once, giving O(V+E) time. Initialize visited to false before the first call and add a distance or parent array when the problem needs shortest paths.",
    "2D Grid BFS (Maze Shortest Path & Queen/Knight Moves)": "Read the grid, locate S and E, then choose the movement array that matches the problem. BFS returns the fewest moves when each allowed move costs one; blocked cells marked # are skipped. Check the required movement rule carefully, because a sliding queen move is one BFS edge regardless of travel distance.",
    "Queen / Rook Move (Grid Ray-Casting BFS)": "Use this ray-casting loop inside a grid BFS to generate every open square reachable in one rook or queen move. Set the direction count to 4 for rook or 8 for queen, and stop a ray at # or the boundary. Enqueue a cell only when its distance improves.",
    "BFS Traversal with Path Printing (Predecessors)": "Call bfs(adj_list,src,n,destination) on an unweighted graph. It records distance and predecessor for each reached vertex, then prints the source-to-destination path. Check the unreachable case before backtracking; vertex IDs must match the adjacency list's indexing.",
    "Topological Sort (Kahn's BFS)": "Call topo_sort(n,adj) on a directed graph with vertices 0..n-1. It repeatedly removes zero-indegree vertices and returns an order in which every edge points forward. A result shorter than n signals a directed cycle, so verify its size before using it as a DAG order.",
    "Bipartite Graph Check & 2-Coloring (BFS)": "Call is_bipartite(n,adj,color) for a 1-indexed undirected graph. It colors every connected component with 0 and 1 and returns false when an edge joins equal colors. On success, color partitions the graph for matching or parity-based reasoning.",
    "Dijkstra (PQ + Path Print)": "Read a 1-indexed undirected weighted graph, choose source s, and run the priority-queue relaxation loop. Nonnegative weights are required. dis[v] is the shortest cost and parent reconstructs the route; this example prints a path from s to vertex n or -1 if unreachable.",
    "Dijkstra Algorithm (Set-Based)": "Fill the global adjacency list with nonnegative weighted edges, then call dijkstra(s,d,p). The function writes shortest distances to d and predecessors to p; unreachable vertices retain INF. It removes stale set entries when a better distance is found, giving O((V+E) log V) time.",
    "0-1 BFS (Deque Shortest Path)": "Call zero_one_bfs(start,n,adj) when every edge weight is exactly 0 or 1. Zero-cost relaxations go to the deque front and unit-cost relaxations to the back, giving O(V+E) time. The returned distance array uses 1e9 for unreachable vertices.",
    "Bellman-Ford Algorithm": "Pass n, directed (u,v,w) edges, and source S. The returned pair contains a flag for a reachable negative cycle and distances from S; unreachable vertices remain BF_INF. Negative weights are allowed, but a flagged cycle makes affected shortest distances undefined.",
    "Negative Cycle Detection & Path Print": "Read directed weighted edges, then perform Bellman-Ford relaxations from the initialization used in the snippet. A vertex relaxed on the nth pass leads to a negative cycle; follow predecessors to enter it and print the cycle. Check indexing and integer range when adapting the example.",
    "Floyd-Warshall (All-Pairs)": "Initialize dist[i][i]=0, edge distances, and INF for missing edges, then run the three nested loops. The resulting dist[i][j] is the shortest cost for every ordered pair when no negative cycle affects it. Time is O(V^3), so reserve it for small graphs.",
    "Tree Diameter via Double BFS": "For an unweighted tree, BFS from any vertex to find one endpoint, then BFS from that endpoint to get the diameter length and other endpoint. The example reads the tree and prints the result. This method assumes connected tree edges; it takes O(V) time.",
    "Lowest Common Ancestor (Binary Lifting)": "Build the rooted tree's depth and binary ancestor table before queries. lca(u,v) returns their lowest common ancestor; the same table supports kth ancestor and edge distance. Size LOGN for the maximum vertex count and use a valid root and indexing convention.",
    "Disjoint Set Union (Union by Size)": "Create DisjointSet(n), call unionBySize(u,v) for each connection, and compare findUPar results to test connectivity. Path compression and union by size make operations nearly constant amortized time. Vertex IDs must lie in the constructor's allocated range.",
    "Fenwick Tree (BIT: Sum & Range Average)": "Create Fenwick(n), add each point value, and use the prefix or range-sum method for inclusive intervals as defined in the struct. An average is sum divided by interval length; cast to floating point for a decimal answer. Updates and queries take O(log n).",
    "Iterative Segment Tree (Range Sum)": "Build the tree from an array, update a point, and query a range sum using the interval convention in its methods. The leaves sit in the second half of the array, and each parent stores the sum of its children. Both updates and queries take O(log n).",
    "Segment Tree for MIN & MAX (Minimal Changes)": "Adapt the preceding segment tree by changing its neutral value and combine operation: +infinity/min for a minimum query, or -infinity/max for a maximum query. Use the same build, point-update, and range-query interval convention. A wrong neutral value corrupts empty-range results.",
    "KACTL Segment Tree (Associative f, [b, e))": "Choose T, the associative combine function f, and identity unit before constructing Tree. update sets one zero-indexed element; query(b,e) combines the half-open range [b,e). This generic form supports sum, min, max, or gcd when the identity matches the operation.",
    "Lazy Segment Tree (Range Add, Set & Max)": "Build LazySegTree from the initial array. Use range_add or range_set on half-open [L,R), then range_max on the same convention. Pending set and add tags let each operation run in O(log n); keep the tag composition order when modifying the code.",
    "Sparse Table (O(1) Static RMQ / GCD)": "Construct the table from a fixed array and an idempotent combine operation such as min, max, or gcd. A query overlaps two power-of-two blocks to answer in O(1) after O(n log n) preprocessing. Rebuild after changes; this structure does not support point updates.",
    "Knuth-Morris-Pratt (KMP) Algorithm": "Build the pattern's LPS prefix table, then scan the text to find every occurrence in O(text length + pattern length). On mismatch, LPS skips characters already known to match. Decide whether the problem wants overlapping matches and handle an empty pattern separately.",
    "Trie (Prefix Tree) & Binary Trie (Max XOR)": "Use the character trie for lowercase a-z words, prefixes, and frequency counts; initialize it before insert/search calls. Use the binary trie for fixed-width integer bits and greedy maximum-XOR queries. Reserve enough nodes and keep the bit width consistent with the input type.",
    "Bitmasking Search (Apple Division)": "Enumerate every subset mask to split the input values into two groups and minimize the absolute sum difference. Bit i says whether value i joins the chosen group. This O(n 2^n) approach is practical only for small n; use a wider sum type for large values.",
    "Two Pointers & Sliding Window Patterns": "Use opposing pointers on sorted data for pair sums, a moving left boundary for a valid contiguous window, or two forward indices to merge sorted lists. Each pointer only moves forward or inward, so the scans are linear after any required sort. Confirm the window condition is monotone before applying it.",
    "Binary Search on Answer (Integer & Real)": "Write a monotone check(mid) predicate, then choose the smallest valid or largest valid boundary according to the template. Integer search keeps an inclusive candidate range; real search repeats enough iterations for the required precision. Test both ends first so the answer is known to lie in the range.",
    "0-1 Knapsack & Unbounded Knapsack": "For each item, update dp capacities downward when it can be chosen once (0-1 knapsack) or upward when it can be reused (unbounded). dp[w] stores the best value for capacity w under the chosen initialization. Complexity is O(items x capacity), so capacity must be manageable.",
    "Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS)": "Call lis(a) to get the length of a strictly increasing subsequence in O(n log n). tails[k] stores the smallest possible ending value for length k+1, and lower_bound replaces it. This returns a length only; track predecessor indices if the actual subsequence is needed.",
    "Kadane's Algorithm (Max Subarray Sum)": "Scan the array once, keeping the best sum ending at the current index and the best interval seen so far. The code records [best_l,best_r] with an inclusive right endpoint. Its negative initialization lets it handle an all-negative array; use a sufficiently wide sum type.",
    "Longest Common Subsequence (LCS)": "Call get_lcs(s,t) to return one longest subsequence present in both strings. The dynamic-programming table computes lengths and backtracking reconstructs a valid answer. Time and memory are O(|s| x |t|), so consider a memory-saving variant for long strings.",
    "Coin Change (Min Coins & Total Ways)": "For a target amount X, the first DP computes the fewest coins and the second counts order-independent combinations modulo MOD. Initialize dp[0] as shown and iterate coins in the intended order. Distinguish unreachable amounts from valid zero-coin answers.",
    "Edit Distance (Levenshtein)": "Call edit_distance(s1,s2) for the minimum number of single-character insertions, deletions, and replacements needed to change s1 into s2. Each DP cell considers those three operations. Time and memory are O(|s1| x |s2|); rows can be compressed if only the distance is needed.",
    "Kruskal's Minimum Spanning Tree (MST)": "Read an undirected weighted graph, sort edges by weight, and use DSU to accept an edge only when it joins different components. The accepted edge sum is the MST cost if exactly V-1 edges are chosen; otherwise the graph is disconnected. Time is O(E log E).",
    "Matrix Exponentiation (O(K^3 log N))": "Represent a linear transition as a K x K matrix, then raise it to power N by repeated squaring. Multiply the resulting matrix by the initial state to obtain the state after N steps. Set the modulus or numeric type appropriately and keep matrix dimensions consistent.",
    "Shoelace Formula (Polygon Area)": "Pass polygon vertices in boundary order, clockwise or counterclockwise. The function sums cross products of consecutive vertices and returns the absolute area. It handles concave simple polygons, but self-intersections need separate interpretation and floating coordinates may introduce rounding error.",
    "2D Point, Vectors & Products": "Use Pt subtraction to form vectors, dot for projection or angle, and cross for orientation and signed area. A positive cross product means a counterclockwise turn in standard Cartesian coordinates. Use a tolerance for floating comparisons or integer coordinates for exact predicates.",
    "Lines, Segments & Triangles Equations": "Choose the helper for point-to-line distance, line intersection, or triangle measures, and pass geometric points in the order shown. Distinguish an infinite line from a finite segment before accepting an intersection. Guard zero-length lines and degenerate triangles to avoid division by zero.",
    "Pick's Theorem, Centroid & 3D Solids": "Use Pick's theorem only for lattice polygons, with area and boundary lattice-point count known. The centroid and solid formulas require the stated shape and dimensions. Keep units consistent: length, area, and volume scale differently, and degenerate polygons have no ordinary area centroid.",
    "Math Series, Logarithms & Algebra": "Select a closed form only after checking its assumptions, such as a nonzero denominator, valid logarithm base, or correct range of n. These formulas replace loops with constant-time arithmetic. Use wide integer types or floating precision where intermediate products can overflow or lose accuracy.",
    "Strongly connected components": "Build the directed graph with zero-indexed vertices, then call the SCC routine once. It assigns a component ID to every vertex; vertices share an ID exactly when each can reach the other. Contracting components produces a DAG for later processing, in O(V+E) time.",
    "2-SAT": "Create a Boolean variable for each choice and add every clause as (x=value OR y=value). The solver builds implications and uses SCC; it returns an assignment when satisfiable, otherwise reports failure. Call the SCC-based routine from this section with the required namespace or copy its dependency.",
    "Bridges in an undirected multigraph": "Give each undirected edge one unique ID and add both adjacency directions with that ID. The DFS low-link test returns edges whose removal increases the component count. Parallel edges are handled by IDs; recursion can exhaust the stack on a very deep graph.",
    "Dinic maximum flow": "Construct the flow network with a vertex count, then add directed edges with nonnegative capacities. Call maxflow(source,sink) to get the maximum total flow. Add both directed edges explicitly for an undirected-capacity model; the reverse residual edge is an internal bookkeeping edge.",
    "Hopcroft–Karp matching": "Number left and right partitions separately from zero, add each allowed left-to-right edge, then run the matching routine. It returns the maximum number of disjoint pairs and exposes the matched partner arrays. Complexity is O(E sqrt(V)) for a bipartite graph.",
    "Undirected Euler walk": "Add each undirected edge once with a unique ID, including parallel edges or loops, then request the walk. A valid result uses every edge exactly once; no path is returned when degree or connectivity conditions fail. Choose the start vertex according to the number of odd-degree vertices.",
    "Mo's algorithm": "Store offline inclusive [l,r] queries with their original indices, then pass matching add, remove, and current-answer callbacks. The loop reorders queries so a moving window changes incrementally. The callbacks must exactly reverse each other; this is useful when updates are absent and range answers are cheap to maintain.",
    "Hungarian minimum-cost assignment": "Pass a rectangular cost matrix with no more rows than columns. The result contains the minimum total cost and the zero-indexed chosen column for each row; negative costs are allowed. Every row receives a distinct column, and the algorithm takes O(n^2 m) time.",
    "Z function": "Call z_function(s) to get, for every index i, the length of the longest prefix matching s[i..]. z[0] is set to zero here. Use it for pattern matching by joining pattern, a separator absent from the input, and text; the scan takes O(n).",
    "Manacher palindrome radii": "Call the routine on a string to obtain odd- and even-centered palindrome radii in O(n). Convert a radius to its actual substring length using the formulas in the code. This avoids testing all centers with quadratic expansion.",
    "Aho–Corasick multiple-pattern matching": "Insert all lowercase a-z patterns, build failure links once, then scan the text. The automaton counts overlapping matches and duplicate patterns as specified by its counters. Rebuild if patterns change, and normalize or reject characters outside a-z.",
    "Suffix array and LCP": "Build the suffix array to list starting positions in lexicographic suffix order; LCP gives common-prefix lengths of adjacent suffixes. Use these arrays for substring order, repeated substrings, or distinct-substring counting. The sorting-based construction is O(n log^2 n) and uses extra memory.",
    "Static double rolling hash": "Build hashes and powers for a fixed string, then query a half-open substring [l,r) in O(1). Two moduli reduce accidental collisions but do not prove equality; compare actual text when correctness requires certainty. Rebuild after edits to the string.",
    "Extended GCD and general CRT": "Combine congruences even when their moduli are not coprime. The routine returns no solution if residues conflict or the combined modulus overflows 64 bits; otherwise it returns a normalized residue and modulus. Supply positive moduli and use the included extended-GCD helper.",
    "Linear Euler totient sieve": "Precompute phi[x] for all x up to the chosen bound in O(n). phi[x] counts integers in [1,x] coprime to x, with phi[1]=1. Call the build routine before queries and size the arrays for the largest x.",
    "64-bit Miller–Rabin": "Call the deterministic uint64 primality test for a nonnegative 64-bit integer. The fixed bases make the result exact over this range, while __int128 handles modular products. This implementation needs GNU C++17; do not pass a negative signed value through an unintended conversion.",
    "Exact integer geometry": "Use integer points for exact orientation, segment intersection, convex hull, signed twice-area, and point-in-polygon tests. The cross product widens to __int128 to protect ordinary 64-bit coordinate products. Check each function's boundary convention, especially whether collinear or edge points count as inside.",
    "Circle–line intersection": "Pass circle center and radius plus two distinct points A and B defining an infinite line. The function returns zero, one, or two intersection points; a degenerate line returns none. Results use long double and a relative tolerance, so compare near-tangent cases carefully.",
}
